            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> Thanks for the issue!\r\n> \r\n> Matching in 'mini.ai' is done with [Lua patterns](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/41487016b71506769c45afac1aee41a180e57fae/doc/mini-ai.txt#L13). The `%b` special pattern item does not seem to work with multibyte characters. You can check it by executing `:=string.find('⟨abc⟩', \"%b⟨⟩\")`, which will print `nil` indicating that no match is found. In turn, plain `<>` works: `=string.find('<abc>', \"%b<>\")` gives `1, 5`.\r\n> \r\n> Closing as not not supported by Lua patterns.\r\n\r\nBonus question: any idea why using `⟨.-⟩` for the unbalanced version always removes the brackets, even when using `div`? Could it be that the \"inside\" still deletes all but one byte of the bracket or something similar? Would I have to use something different for the second argument (other than `^.().*().$`)",
